From aaa76e753cbdf5e84b1c7ede16191d4aa130af2c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 13:49:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] iOS: drive a physical iPhone through the macOS iPhone
 Mirroring window
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Android has adb, so `computeruse.android` talks to the device: screencap
returns the framebuffer, `input tap` injects a real touch. iOS has no such
channel here — Appium / WebDriverAgent / XCUITest are absent from this
workspace and simctl is simulator-only with no tap. What macOS 15+ does give
is a window showing the live phone screen that forwards pointer and keyboard
events to it, so the driver is assembled from parts that already worked:
screencapture -R over the window rect, cliclick inside it, System Events for
keys.

Two corrections separate this from the Android case, and both are silent when
wrong — a tap lands somewhere plausible rather than failing:

  - the model's coordinates index a *crop*, so the content origin has to be
    added back; dropping it offsets every tap by the window's desktop position
  - the scale is points-per-model-pixel and comes from the window rect in
    POINTS; deriving it from the Retina capture's pixels halves it, which
    reads as "everything lands in the top-left quadrant"

Both are pinned by tests, using a window that is neither at the origin nor
square so either mistake changes the answers. Verified the gate falls: dropping
the origin fails 10 assertions, removing the swipe's intermediate motion
samples fails 4.

All the geometry, gesture-path and key-script logic sits above the #?(:clj)
boundary and is therefore tested on cljs too; only the shell-outs are JVM-only
and those are untested here — they need a Mac, a paired iPhone and two granted
permissions.

Deliberate mappings, since none of them are forced: right_click is a
press-and-hold (iOS's actual secondary gesture, there being no secondary
click); scroll direction is finger travel, matching computeruse.android
because two device drivers in one library disagreeing would be worse than
either convention being the surprising one; key "home"/"appswitcher"/
"spotlight" reach iPhone Mirroring's own cmd-1/2/3, which is how the phone is
navigated at all.

What it cannot do is stated in the namespace docstring rather than discovered:
no multitouch (one pointer), no frame-accurate timing (live video stream), and
it stops when someone picks the phone up.

clojure -M:test: 30 tests, 121 assertions, 0 failures (baseline 17/70).
clojure -M:lint: 0 errors; the one new warning is the #?(:clj) false positive
that computeruse.macos and computeruse.android already produce.
The README's test count was already stale at 13/60; corrected to the measured
value.

